# mrm-trace

A Python research package for collecting, parsing, labelling, and analysing LLM inference
memory access traces. Designed as scientific instrumentation for
**Managed-Retention Memory (MRM)** research - it characterises how model weights, KV cache,
activations, and runtime allocations are actually accessed during inference.

**Primary metrics:** retention duration · write-once ratio · read frequency · working set size

---

## Install from PyPI

```bash
pip install mrm-trace
```

> Linux (or WSL2) is required for `perf mem` collection. See [Requirements](#requirements) below.

---

## Requirements

| Requirement | Notes |
|---|---|
| Linux (WSL2 supported) | memray works everywhere; `perf mem` requires bare-metal or PMU-capable VM |
| Python ≥ 3.11 | Tested on 3.11 and 3.12 |
| sudo / root | Required for `native_traces=True` (memray) and `perf mem` |

### Collector capability by environment

| Environment | Best collector | `region_map` | Timestamps | Cache level |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| WSL2 (non-root) | `memray` | empty | 0 | n/a |
| WSL2 (root) | `memray --native-traces` | empty † | 0 | n/a |
| Bare-metal Linux (root) | `perf mem` (spawn mode) | populated ‡ | nanoseconds | L1/L2/L3/DRAM |
| Cloud VM / Colab | `memray` | empty | 0 | n/a |
| Debian bare-metal (root) | `perf mem` (spawn mode) | populated ‡ | nanoseconds | L1/L2/L3/DRAM |

**WSL2:** The Microsoft WSL2 kernel does not expose hardware PMU counters.
`perf mem` will not produce data. Use `memray` for development; run on bare-metal for publication data.

**perf mem — spawn mode:** `perf mem record -p PID` (attach to running process) fails on
Ubuntu 24.04 / kernel 6.8 for Intel PEBS load-latency events even as root. Use spawn mode:
`perf mem record -- python script.py`. Both `demo_qwen_hf.py` and `demo_perf_mem.py` use spawn
mode — perf launches the script itself (via `--headless`) so the traced process starts under
perf from the first instruction.

**`native_traces`:** memray `native_traces=True` requires root. pip-installed `llama-cpp-python`
strips C symbols in older versions, so `region_map` remains empty via memray (`†`). For a
populated region map, use `demo_perf_mem.py` with `llama-cpp-python ≥ 0.3.34`, which exports
`ggml_*` symbols from its split shared libraries without a debug build.

**region_map with PyTorch (‡):** `perf mem` populates `region_map` only with llama.cpp (GGUF)
models. PyTorch/transformers symbols (`at::native::*`) do not match the `ggml_*` labeller rules.
For PyTorch runs, `region_type = unknown` for all records — retention and write-once metrics
are still valid but undifferentiated by region type.

**Timestamps:** memray does not record per-allocation timestamps. `timestamp_ns = 0` and
`retention_p99_s = 0` in all memray runs. Only `perf mem` provides real nanosecond hardware timestamps.

**sudo:** `sudo -E python` resolves to the **system Python**. Always use:
```bash
sudo -E venv/bin/python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py
```

---

## Install

```bash
# Clone and set up a virtual environment
git clone https://github.com/DhiSys-AI/MRM-Trace
cd MRM-Trace
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate      # Windows WSL: same command

# Install package + test dependencies
pip install -e ".[test]"

# Optional: install matplotlib/seaborn for figures
pip install -e ".[test,plots]"
```

---

## Quick start

```bash
# Day-one machine check: can this box collect? (exit 0 = publication-grade perf mem)
sudo mrm-trace validate --machine

# Validate a config file
mrm-trace validate --config config/default_experiment.yaml

# Preview what a run would do (dry run)
mrm-trace plan --config config/default_experiment.yaml

# Run a full experiment (requires model files + sudo for perf)
mrm-trace run --config config/default_experiment.yaml
```

---

## Live demo scripts

End-to-end scripts that run real inference against small models and write all mrm-trace
artifacts to a timestamped results directory. Located in [`notebooks/scripts/`](notebooks/scripts/).

### Setup

```bash
# From the repo root (WSL2 or Linux)
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[test]"
pip install memray
```

### TinyLlama 1.1B (llama-cpp-python + GGUF)

```bash
# Install backend
pip install llama-cpp-python \
  --extra-index-url https://abetlen.github.io/llama-cpp-python/whl/cpu

# Download model (~670 MB, one-time)
mkdir -p models
wget -P models/ https://huggingface.co/TheBloke/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0-GGUF/resolve/main/tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf

# Run (non-root — Python-level trace only)
python notebooks/scripts/demo_tinyllama.py \
  --model models/tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf

# Run as root — enables native_traces=True (C-level symbols, populated region_map)
sudo -E python notebooks/scripts/demo_tinyllama.py \
  --model models/tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf \
  --native-traces
```

### Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (transformers, no GGUF needed)

```bash
# Install backend (model auto-downloads from HuggingFace, ~1 GB)
pip install transformers torch accelerate

# Auto-detects collector: memray on WSL2/non-root, perf mem on bare-metal Linux as root
python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py

# Force memray (any environment):
python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py --collector memray

# Force perf mem (bare-metal Linux, run as root with full venv path):
sudo -E venv/bin/python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py --collector perf-mem

# Larger variant
python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct
```

> **Note on region_map:** The Qwen demo uses PyTorch/transformers. `region_map` will be empty
> and all records will have `region_type = unknown` regardless of collector. Retention and
> write-once metrics are still measured correctly but cannot be separated by region type.
> For classified regions, use `demo_perf_mem.py` with a GGUF model.

### perf mem + TinyLlama GGUF (bare-metal Linux, classified regions)

```bash
# Requires root and hardware PMU — does NOT work on WSL2
# Use full venv path: sudo -E python resolves to system Python, not venv
sudo -E venv/bin/python notebooks/scripts/demo_perf_mem.py \
  --model models/tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf
```

`llama-cpp-python ≥ 0.3.34` ships split shared libraries (`libllama.so`, `libggml.so`) with
1,334 `ggml_*` and 281 `llama_kv_cache*` exported symbols — no debug build required.
The standard `pip install llama-cpp-python` is sufficient.

After each run, the pipeline prints an inline quality gate before the labelling step:

```
  Trace quality gate [perf_mem] — PASS
    Rows parsed          : 342,162
    access_latency_ns    : [████████████████████] 100.0%
    cache_level          : [████████████████████] 100.0%
    symbol (non-empty)   : [░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░]   0.0%   ← expected for heap allocations
    region classified    : [░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░]   0.0%   ← expected without alloc events
```

`FAIL` exits immediately — the run is never exported to disk.

### Script options

| Flag | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `--model PATH` | varies | GGUF path or HuggingFace model ID |
| `--ctx N` | 2048 | Context length |
| `--tokens N` | 128 (perf_mem/tinyllama), 256 (qwen) | **Max output tokens per prompt** — recorded in `manifest.csv` as `max_tokens_per_prompt` |
| `--batch-size N` | 1 | Number of prompts in the batch |
| `--rep N` | 0 | Repetition index (0 = warmup; 1+ = data runs) |
| `--out DIR` | `results/` | Output base directory |
| `--machine SLUG` | hostname | Machine identifier used as `results/<machine>/` subdirectory |
| `--collector auto\|memray\|perf-mem` | `auto` | Override collector selection (Qwen demo only) |
| `--native-traces` | auto (root check) | Force `native_traces=True` for memray |
| `--no-native-traces` | — | Force `native_traces=False` |

**Setting your own token limit:**
```bash
# Longer generations — more KV cache growth, longer retention windows
python notebooks/scripts/demo_qwen_hf.py --tokens 512

# Short test run
sudo -E venv/bin/python notebooks/scripts/demo_perf_mem.py \
  --model models/tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf --tokens 32
```
The ceiling you pass is saved as `max_tokens_per_prompt` in `manifest.csv` and as
`generation.output_tokens` in `metadata.json`. The actual tokens the model generated
(may be less if EOS fires early) appear separately as `total_output_tokens` in `manifest.csv`.

All scripts run **7 prompts** (P1–P7: general history topics, a long-form Africa essay for
sustained KV growth, and a Roman Empire follow-up for KV reuse measurement) and write
`trace.parquet`, `region_map.parquet`, `kv_block_lifecycle.parquet`, `metrics.csv`,
`metadata.json`, and `manifest.json` to `results/<machine_id>/<model_id>/<run_id>/`.
A dataset-level `results/manifest.csv` accumulates one summary row per run across all machines.

